  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Cap-d'Ail, a small town squeezed between Monaco and Nice, has a pebble beach at the Pointe des Douaniers that the town hall calls "the dog beach", because dogs are tolerated there. A setting typical of this stretch of the Riviera, with views over the sea and the surrounding rocks.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The town hall (cap-dail.fr, "Bord de mer" page) places this beach west of the point, on the coastal path, and advises parking at the Cap Fleuri car park. The regional tourist office, for its part, gives the address "east of the cape" and states that dogs must be kept on a leash on the path leading to the cove, then may run free and swim once there. The two sources therefore disagree on the exact orientation: look for the signs on-site. It is the only beach in the town where dogs are tolerated — handy if you're based in Monaco, whose guide listing records no beach for dogs.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the coastal path, open to dogs on a leash according to the town hall, is a lovely walk before or after the swim.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The town hall reminds visitors that the coastal promenade is closed by municipal staff in strong wind or rough seas. Other dog beaches exist in Roquebrune-Cap-Martin and Menton, less than 20 minutes away by car.</p>
    </div>

